    {
      "id": 224696,
      "name": "MindWanderer",
      "username": "MindWanderer",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mindwanderer/{size}/4816_2.png",
      "created_at": "2018-12-17T21:56:46.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Yeah, when I run games in established settings, I always make sure to avoid using established characters (even if the players don't think I'm playing them \"wrong\", I always feel like I'm doing the authors injustice), and to write the story in such a way that knowing the setting's history isn't helpful at all.&nbsp; Even then it can be tricky once you reach high-level threats--if something is going to destroy all Faerun, why aren't the epic-level heroes like Elminster getting involved?&nbsp; I vastly prefer to design my own settings using concepts and models from existing ones, unless the game's default setting exists only in the game and doesn't have any lore any of us could be familiar with.</p>\n<p>If I were to run a Sentinels game, it would be either a Disparation universe, or cover obscure events in the past that could reasonably be unknown to SC fans.</p>",
      "post_number": 3,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:58:08.961Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
